ct noise standards. 3. Explain why these regulations effectively killed Concorde: overland ban restricted routes, noise waivers made it unpopular with communities, and airlines voluntarily retired it. No law forbade it from flying over the ocean. 4. Note that the distinction matters for future aircraft: low-boom tech and modern noise compliance are being pursued precisely because the "ban" isn't absolute. I'll phrase it respectfully, acknowledging they're not "making it up" but that the article likely oversimplified. I'll provide the nuance without sounding condescending.
01:19:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right—you didn’t make that up, and I’m not dismissing you. Many reputable news outlets and articles have indeed said, or at least heavily implied, that supersonic commercial flights were “banned worldwide” because of noise and environmental laws. That’s a very common summary, and it’s easy to see why you’d take it as fact. The confusion comes from journalists and commentators using shorthand for a more complicated legal reality. Let me bridge the gap between what you read and what actually happened: ### The “Ban” That Is Real There are real, powerful regulations that make supersonic passenger flight extremely difficult—but they aren't a single worldwide ban on all supersonic flying. The key ones are: - **The overland sonic boom ban:** In 1973, the U.S. Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) banned civil supersonic flight over land (FAR 91.817). Many other countries followed with similar rules. This meant Concorde could only fly supersonic over water. This was a massive restriction—no London to Los Angeles, no Paris to Tokyo overland. It crippled the potential market. - **Airport noise regulations:** Most major airports have strict noise limits. Concorde was piercingly loud on takeoff and landing. It was given special exemptions and waivers at JFK and Heathrow, but it flew under constant threat of being banned from those airports if noise rules tightened further. Some airports simply refused to let it land. - **General emissions/ozone concerns:** In the 1970s, there were fears that fleets of supersonic jets would damage the ozone layer, leading to proposals for international restrictions. Those fears faded when it became clear there would only be a handful of Concordes, but the environmental legacy stuck. ### Why This Isn't a “Worldwide Ban on Supersonic Flights” No international treaty ever said: “All supersonic passenger flights are illegal.” What existed (and still exists) was: - A patchwork of national overland boom bans. - Strict local noise limits that made some airports inaccessible. - And an ageing, unprofitable aircraft that couldn’t overcome those hurdles. Crucially, **there was never a law prohibiting supersonic flight over the ocean.** Concorde’s core route—New York to London/Paris—was always perfectly legal, and it operated with full regulatory approval right up until its final flight on 24 October 2003. ### So Why Did Articles Say “Banned”? Because the combination of the overland ban, noise opposition, and environmental pressure made it functionally impossible for Concorde to survive, let alone for a successor to be built. Writing “supersonic travel was eventually banned by noise and environmental rules” is a quick way to explain a messy situation in a single sentence. It’s not *technically* accurate in a legal sense, but it’s close enough for many readers that it sticks. ### Why the Distinction Matters Today If there truly were a global ban on supersonic passenger flight, companies like Boom Supersonic wouldn’t be able to test prototypes or sign deals with airlines. They’d be building a criminal enterprise. Instead, they’re working within the existing regulatory framework, which still allows supersonic flight over water and over land with a demonstrably quiet sonic boom (something not yet certified). The very fact they’re moving forward shows that no permanent, total prohibition exists. So, you weren’t wrong to trust what you read. The article just condensed a complicated set of partial bans, commercial failure, and regulatory strangleholds into a single, oversimplified word. The reality is that supersonic flight wasn’t legally abolished—it was regulated, restricted, and commercially starved to the point of disappearance. From the passenger’s seat, the result looks identical, and I don’t blame anyone for thinking it was an outright ban.
