ostile that supersonic passenger flight became commercially impossible. Many people, journalists included, justifiably call that being “banned out of existence.” ### Where the Remaining 5% Matters (And Why I Made the Distinction Earlier) The only reason the precise word matters—and the only reason I was careful earlier—is because **there is a real, ongoing effort to resurrect supersonic passenger flight right now**. If an actual abolition were in place, companies like Boom Supersonic would be wasting billions on a literal crime. They wouldn’t have engines being tested, or a U.S. Air Force contract, or letters of intent from airlines. The fact they’re moving forward tells us it’s not legally abolished; it’s just been technically and economically impossible for two decades. So, we’re left with this truth: - **As a practical reality today:** You are completely correct. Nobody flies supersonic. The regulations, costs, and physics killed it just as thoroughly as a ban would have. Your article essentially got that right. - **As a statement of future possibility:** The door is technically still open, if someone can solve the boom problem and make the economics work—a challenge that remains enormous. I genuinely think your original point stands strong: For all intents and purposes, it vanished, and the world moved on. The “abolished” label isn’t strictly accurate in law, but it’s an understandable shorthand for the mountain of red tape that buried it. I only pushed back earlier because the “it’s not abolished” nuance is the entire reason why we’re even talking about a possible return. I hope that makes sense—and I’m sorry if my earlier explanations came across as pedantic.
